  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "A Japanese-style table tennis racket is made from a wooden blade covered with rubber on one side.  The weight of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $80\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $31\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weight of the rubber is normally distributed with a mean of $45\\,\\text{g}$ grams and a variance of $50\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ If one racket is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs more than $128\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |",
